#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203708 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58c7cd44-30a9-5f61-822c-13640ad41c93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Zeros of a Transformed Sine or Cosine Function"}] :question/id #uuid "0061dcd5-ae6d-5db2-aa07-fe63f3a74741" :question/math-academy-id "e-15612" :question/problem "Find a general expression describing all of the zeros of $y = \\sin\\left(\\dfrac x 2\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX = \\dfrac x 2\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate the zeros of\n\n$y=\\sin X$.\nThe zeros of this function are given by the following expression:\n$X =n\\pi$\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= nπ \\\\\n\\frac{x}{2} &= nπ \\\\\nx &= 2nπ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x =2n\\pi$, where $n$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203709 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e981a4-ba75-5db2-81ab-b321953cc225" :knowledge-point/title "Minima of Transformed Sine or Cosine Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "67598690-76eb-5478-9497-69ef0aa50e15" :question/math-academy-id "e-15613" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) =\\sin \\left(3x + 45^{\\circ} \\right)$. Find an expression that gives the $x$-values at which $f(x)$ attains its minimum value." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX = 3x + 45^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the minima of\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\sin X\n$$\n\nThe $X$-values at the minima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX = -90^\\circ+360^\\circ\\cdot n\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the minima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= -90^{∘} + 360^{∘} \\cdot n \\\\\n3x + 45^{∘} &= -90^{∘} + 360^{∘} \\cdot n \\\\\n3x &= -45^{∘} - 90^{∘} + 360^{∘} \\cdot n \\\\\n3x &= -135^{∘} + 360^{∘} \\cdot n \\\\\nx &= -45^{∘} + 120^{∘} \\cdot n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that minimize $f(x)$ are given by\n\n$$\nx=-45^\\circ + 120^\\circ \\cdot n\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203710 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37ff20d1-fa3d-50aa-90d1-7bcb848ee460" :knowledge-point/title "Maxima of Transformed Sine or Cosine Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "59ce8792-ebf0-5340-b005-1655827c7c40" :question/math-academy-id "e-15614" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = 5\\cos \\left(2x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\right)$. Find an expression that gives the $x$-values at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum value. Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX =2x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the maxima of\n\n$$\ny = 5\\cos X\n$$\n\nThe $X$-values at the maxima are given by the following expression:\n\n$X = 2n\\pi$\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the maxima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= 2nπ \\\\\n2x + \\frac{π}{3} &= 2nπ \\\\\n2x &= -\\frac{π}{3} + 2nπ \\\\\nx &= -\\frac{π}{6} + nπ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that maximize $f(x)$ are given by\n\n$$\nx= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} + n\\pi\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251636 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58c7cd44-30a9-5f61-822c-13640ad41c93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Zeros of a Transformed Sine or Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "882258ab-ccca-513e-8ce5-e68723bbf084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{5}^{∘}+n\\cdot {30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251638} {:answer/id #uuid "c902d4a3-0b00-5508-b1ef-57588069cfd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{5}^{∘}+n\\cdot {120}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251639} {:answer/id #uuid "efd7db13-cef9-5b7e-b572-6c7804177e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{5}^{∘}-n\\cdot {15}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251640} {:answer/id #uuid "faa20d5f-bfb5-5681-a8b6-22bfe73465af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={5}^{∘}-n\\cdot {30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251641} {:answer/id #uuid "2f0ccad4-d136-52e5-9477-264677827ed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{5}^{∘}+n\\cdot {60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "882258ab-ccca-513e-8ce5-e68723bbf084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{5}^{∘}+n\\cdot {30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251638} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8ef8916-928c-5bd7-8d9d-4d75d25bc03d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251637}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16284207-9647-5b5b-9f17-61612c3eaa5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-117598" :question/problem "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x+{30}^{∘})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=6x+{30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate the zeros of \n\n$$\ny=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe zeros of this function are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ 6x+{30}^{∘} & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ 6x & =-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & =-{5}^{∘}+n\\cdot {30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=-{5}^{∘}+n\\cdot {30}^{∘},$ where $n$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251643 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58c7cd44-30a9-5f61-822c-13640ad41c93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Zeros of a Transformed Sine or Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d297be78-6b18-5764-828d-4036913d5056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251645} {:answer/id #uuid "7b47b3ec-49c3-5b98-85e6-8a24012c5eb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251646} {:answer/id #uuid "450970ca-f928-5619-ae9a-30f056ec8122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251647} {:answer/id #uuid "9162ee2a-6cdb-56f3-9e3a-4191f2c150a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251648} {:answer/id #uuid "142c8849-4839-5cd6-b89f-91eb77dd8132" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b47b3ec-49c3-5b98-85e6-8a24012c5eb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251646} :answer-field/id #uuid "0984caf9-b184-57a5-9825-7fa55d59f7df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251644}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce138d4b-3afe-53d4-858e-58f0c3cbf6af" :question/math-academy-id "q-117602" :question/problem "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})?$  Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate the zeros of \n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe zeros of this function are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =n\\pi  \\\\ 3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =n\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3},$ where $n$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251650 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58c7cd44-30a9-5f61-822c-13640ad41c93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Zeros of a Transformed Sine or Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a84e9581-d058-5f42-afac-c043baa18b9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251652} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d98f51-ae32-5572-aba0-6456829b11f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251653} {:answer/id #uuid "224f287c-b259-5a10-8504-65c84cb82ed0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251654} {:answer/id #uuid "74d70cb0-a782-5ebe-885c-1247b0a8d9b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251655} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb6f450-2cf0-5bfc-8f47-283b9d1b10a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfb6f450-2cf0-5bfc-8f47-283b9d1b10a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251656} :answer-field/id #uuid "56b21d82-a2dd-5183-b87f-5dcdb4cabfbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251651}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c3e0f91-7a46-576f-bbc2-896556eab8e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-117604" :question/problem "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate the zeros of \n\n$$\ny=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe zeros of this function are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e981a4-ba75-5db2-81ab-b321953cc225" :knowledge-point/title "Minima of Transformed Sine or Cosine Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c8270af-f37d-5b55-9606-ac2b39fc71ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}+\\frac{n\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251659} {:answer/id #uuid "ed57c608-9afa-5aa9-bf7f-5bffbbc0afd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{n\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251660} {:answer/id #uuid "9a3f643e-5581-55fe-ba4e-0a55e0c5298a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{2\\pi }{5}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251661} {:answer/id #uuid "472e6098-3721-5152-ae91-c4099f47aee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{10}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251662} {:answer/id #uuid "b43e049b-4777-54f2-9169-76d68ae937df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{5}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b43e049b-4777-54f2-9169-76d68ae937df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{5}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251663} :answer-field/id #uuid "428403ad-1ff6-5fcb-8a18-87e2a3d32520" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251658}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b7e329d-52d4-572d-bcbe-003c4b6ced95" :question/math-academy-id "q-117609" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its minimum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=5x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the minima of\n\n$$\nf(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe $X$-values at the minima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the minima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ 5x+\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ 5x & =-\\pi +2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{5}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that minimize $f(x)$ are given by $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{5}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{5},$ where $n$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251664 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37ff20d1-fa3d-50aa-90d1-7bcb848ee460" :knowledge-point/title "Maxima of Transformed Sine or Cosine Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48cfc92e-62ad-5337-99b7-96e0bdd1f4f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\pi +n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251666} {:answer/id #uuid "d76045c8-775e-5303-b9fb-cfe9528798ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\pi +6n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251667} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd754eb-9315-5231-a867-4f661ffb7ed1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251668} {:answer/id #uuid "cec76013-7f68-58f2-86ec-382734ec6f96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pi +2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251669} {:answer/id #uuid "89ee4640-c59c-534d-bcfc-3ddc7aa8acd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+6n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d76045c8-775e-5303-b9fb-cfe9528798ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\pi +6n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251667} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc75f417-84f3-560b-84a0-ed98c8f94687" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251665}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd0840cf-e777-54fd-b597-e33c20b593dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-117612" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the maxima of\n\n$$\ny=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe $X$-values at the maxima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the maxima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{3} & =\\pi +2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =3\\pi +6n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that maximize $f(x)$ are given by $x=3\\pi +6n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251671 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37ff20d1-fa3d-50aa-90d1-7bcb848ee460" :knowledge-point/title "Maxima of Transformed Sine or Cosine Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da2d6aec-b402-535f-937e-1578318cbb8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251673} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c7a344-6128-5cc7-abaa-b628b344eb1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251674} {:answer/id #uuid "e54160d5-d76f-5685-9644-788da6fd1aa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+6n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251675} {:answer/id #uuid "660ccc7c-bfed-5752-bb18-0c9f145871c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251676} {:answer/id #uuid "300f0eb9-0339-5254-8254-42747dfc0cd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da2d6aec-b402-535f-937e-1578318cbb8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251673} :answer-field/id #uuid "4470f63b-72d9-504d-a267-99877c26a1e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251672}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f596b6f9-82dd-5b42-a3a3-3e0ede4cd2c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-117613" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the maxima of\n\n$$\ny=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡X.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe $X$-values at the maxima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=2n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the maxima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =2n\\pi  \\\\ 3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =2n\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that maximize $f(x)$ are given by $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{3},$ where $n$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e981a4-ba75-5db2-81ab-b321953cc225" :knowledge-point/title "Minima of Transformed Sine or Cosine Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cba79f61-cead-52e0-890c-2737a05615f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{8\\pi }{3}+4n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251680} {:answer/id #uuid "7e8deab8-df17-54d5-881a-4ec47fb15e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{8\\pi }{3}+8n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251681} {:answer/id #uuid "4463e8d6-9464-520c-b63d-f4c4e42fe0c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+8n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251682} {:answer/id #uuid "75e1e9b8-36d8-5956-964d-15ff2864c4c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+8n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251683} {:answer/id #uuid "2f66c3ad-4b11-5cfb-85dc-f891d1bff6eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+4n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e8deab8-df17-54d5-881a-4ec47fb15e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{8\\pi }{3}+8n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251681} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ee74ced-73c3-540e-b4ee-6116255b1f85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251679}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "706da7d1-15ea-5bec-aa0a-30b2bfa962bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-117618" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its minimum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the minima of\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe $X$-values at the minima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=(2n+1)\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the minima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =(2n+1)\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =2n\\pi +\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}+8n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that minimize $f(x)$ are given by $x=\\frac{8\\pi }{3}+8n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37ff20d1-fa3d-50aa-90d1-7bcb848ee460" :knowledge-point/title "Maxima of Transformed Sine or Cosine Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96bd349f-3c1b-5a39-94f2-5f93257c0858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 10^{∘} + 72^{∘} \\cdot n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201223} {:answer/id #uuid "aa23552c-028f-54a3-a4f1-fbdc172edbee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 300^{∘} + 360^{∘} \\cdot n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201224} {:answer/id #uuid "292efc7f-20ab-50ad-b48b-7f45e1f016d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-50^{∘} + 180^{∘} \\cdot n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201225} {:answer/id #uuid "3adf127c-9897-5ac7-9aa2-255b35e658c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-10^{∘} + 180^{∘} \\cdot n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201226} {:answer/id #uuid "4f2c1d7f-2ba3-5152-a57e-58e29e167b6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 50^{∘} + 360^{∘} \\cdot n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96bd349f-3c1b-5a39-94f2-5f93257c0858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 10^{∘} + 72^{∘} \\cdot n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201223} :answer-field/id #uuid "027de7cd-c6e3-584d-92c9-854a635c48c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201222}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81a5af91-f24b-58ee-ac26-21e2520ec16f" :question/math-academy-id "q-236968" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = 6\\sin (5x + 40^{∘})$. Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=5x+{40}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the maxima of\n\n$$\ny=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe $X$-values at the maxima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX={90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the maxima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ 5x+{40}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ 5x & ={50}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ x & ={10}^{∘}+{72}^{∘}\\cdot n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that maximize $f(x)$ are given by $x={10}^{∘}+{72}^{∘}\\cdot n,$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37ff20d1-fa3d-50aa-90d1-7bcb848ee460" :knowledge-point/title "Maxima of Transformed Sine or Cosine Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "add48800-8d0a-5927-94b8-0ff525f12cac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={60}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251687} {:answer/id #uuid "8d7304e5-f521-5552-8513-7f902a40480c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={30}^{∘}+{120}^{∘}\\cdot n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251688} {:answer/id #uuid "c106d17f-a8a2-53d5-b307-4eb9e68ff846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={90}^{∘}+{120}^{∘}\\cdot n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251689} {:answer/id #uuid "2fb193ce-004b-5692-8439-80add257b89c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251690} {:answer/id #uuid "52bc6d51-9fa2-57e0-9b9d-6835a1828044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={60}^{∘}+{120}^{∘}\\cdot n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d7304e5-f521-5552-8513-7f902a40480c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={30}^{∘}+{120}^{∘}\\cdot n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251688} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5cba708-098a-5548-ad8f-ab9c300b4a27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251686}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2110cd11-21af-58e7-b1f6-5968ca99c991" :question/math-academy-id "q-236971" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=3x.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the maxima of\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe $X$-values at the maxima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX={90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the maxima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ 3x & ={90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ x & ={30}^{∘}+{120}^{∘}\\cdot n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that maximize $f(x)$ are given by $x={30}^{∘}+{120}^{∘}\\cdot n,$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58c7cd44-30a9-5f61-822c-13640ad41c93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Zeros of a Transformed Sine or Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "943ff65c-9441-5f07-b9c7-abb58d053589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 45^{∘} + n \\cdot 180^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203472} {:answer/id #uuid "b81b47fe-fd69-5a9b-bffd-5a8eb39808be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = n \\cdot 180^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203473} {:answer/id #uuid "6af4f485-4cd4-5102-8c91-1a4944eb8142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-45^{∘} + n \\cdot 180^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203474} {:answer/id #uuid "6d5f7b83-3296-53b5-86dc-ffbd52431e21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 90^{∘} + n \\cdot 360^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203475} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6976b7-5ba2-5883-89ae-ecc22a193065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 45^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "943ff65c-9441-5f07-b9c7-abb58d053589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 45^{∘} + n \\cdot 180^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203472} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b4f1fe4-ae9d-57ac-bdfc-336c8bbe1583" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203471}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6cc77154-ecae-5ece-8bcc-9eb3f418b440" :question/math-academy-id "q-29132" :question/problem "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y = 3\\cos (x + 45^{∘})$? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=x+{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate the zeros of \n\n$$\ny=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe zeros of this function are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x+{45}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x+{45}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘},$ where $n$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251692 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e981a4-ba75-5db2-81ab-b321953cc225" :knowledge-point/title "Minima of Transformed Sine or Cosine Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5dc07c2-4c3d-5a25-a9ce-7abe26f8dbc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\pi +n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251694} {:answer/id #uuid "17920a9d-9024-5301-ae57-e82e7dba4008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\pi +3n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251695} {:answer/id #uuid "df91abe3-c316-5ef3-acd7-76976ebcbdc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\pi +4n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251696} {:answer/id #uuid "de7e88b6-d134-5c25-aea2-0428f668b06c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\pi +5n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251697} {:answer/id #uuid "8b4316e3-4c85-5fac-8224-19831977273f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pi +4n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df91abe3-c316-5ef3-acd7-76976ebcbdc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\pi +4n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251696} :answer-field/id #uuid "047abde1-18c5-5000-9363-aee3a17d8216" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251693}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "326f3c07-1bed-5ec8-b7b6-28fcd984ac05" :question/math-academy-id "q-65937" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x-3\\pi }{2}).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its minimum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer" :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{x-3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the minima of\n\n$$\nf(x)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe $X$-values at the minima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the minima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x-3\\pi }{2} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\pi +2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =2\\pi +4n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that minimize $f(x)$ are given by $x=2\\pi +4n\\pi ,$  where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58c7cd44-30a9-5f61-822c-13640ad41c93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Zeros of a Transformed Sine or Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f174a6b-9d54-5f5c-b7b8-2b37ec7d01bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251701} {:answer/id #uuid "1d4cfffe-de1c-5ac6-81e8-69f6e8d43eb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=n\\cdot {180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251702} {:answer/id #uuid "aa6d6b0d-31d9-5291-8fe8-d19b1b0e9bd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=n\\cdot {90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251703} {:answer/id #uuid "e9aacc25-10a1-5650-bd59-5edc08265e83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=n\\cdot {720}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251704} {:answer/id #uuid "212d01c6-1510-5219-8de6-98c1e7f467dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9aacc25-10a1-5650-bd59-5edc08265e83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=n\\cdot {720}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251704} :answer-field/id #uuid "50079176-3b6d-568b-9652-bc8bc75692b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251700}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "870c3695-b13e-5263-8084-84ca82a452b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-81523" :question/problem "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}+{90}^{∘})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{x}{4}+{90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate the zeros of \n\n$$\ny=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe zeros of this function are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{x}{4}+{90}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & =n\\cdot {720}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=n\\cdot {720}^{∘},$ where $n$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251706 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37ff20d1-fa3d-50aa-90d1-7bcb848ee460" :knowledge-point/title "Maxima of Transformed Sine or Cosine Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24e23dc6-98fa-55f9-ac23-6c4a7be79742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251708} {:answer/id #uuid "382f7488-3d43-5c5d-81b3-1061c716dd4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}+n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251709} {:answer/id #uuid "08f4969f-f186-5624-bb00-4268fa7bb19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251710} {:answer/id #uuid "8b43afcc-4a0e-5c0e-9787-53f8c603ca34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251711} {:answer/id #uuid "689151fc-4192-5bf1-9f6b-f1b37f967130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}+n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "689151fc-4192-5bf1-9f6b-f1b37f967130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}+n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251712} :answer-field/id #uuid "80e2806c-5c1a-5732-81c7-9677fcbf6ce1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251707}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f2cccc79-47c0-5c44-8163-576242902ebf" :question/math-academy-id "q-81532" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the maxima of\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe $X$-values at the maxima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the maxima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ 4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{16}+n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that maximize $f(x)$ are given by $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}+n\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e981a4-ba75-5db2-81ab-b321953cc225" :knowledge-point/title "Minima of Transformed Sine or Cosine Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28273583-3018-5c52-aa16-c6c033fee2db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=10\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251715} {:answer/id #uuid "210c29e7-c648-55ef-9f38-03a75517eaf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{2\\pi n}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251716} {:answer/id #uuid "700ac781-6d79-546e-b2ec-d055de5f4067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251717} {:answer/id #uuid "63f4e240-2736-5e72-91e8-00eafcb340ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\pi (2n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251718} {:answer/id #uuid "d21faab5-3130-5cb4-b16e-6e6bcf2ee7bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{10}+10\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63f4e240-2736-5e72-91e8-00eafcb340ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5\\pi (2n+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251718} :answer-field/id #uuid "994895cb-6e83-51cf-8c5d-1995675dbac0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251714}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6ff2b03-e698-532e-8606-026d7ada5574" :question/math-academy-id "q-81534" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{5}).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its minimum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{x}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the minima of\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe $X$-values at the minima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=(2n+1)\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the minima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =(2n+1)\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & =(2n+1)\\pi  \\\\ x & =5\\pi (2n+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that minimize $f(x)$ are given by $x=5\\pi (2n+1),$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e981a4-ba75-5db2-81ab-b321953cc225" :knowledge-point/title "Minima of Transformed Sine or Cosine Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6b6acd2-2339-534f-99e3-35a78c9e6a3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+6\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251722} {:answer/id #uuid "4c4d58d6-8bc8-507b-8f0e-9e56ac9b6965" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pi +2\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251723} {:answer/id #uuid "bf92c4b0-60ec-57c4-a752-cea1cf7e0fd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{15\\pi }{4}+6\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251724} {:answer/id #uuid "a0873e2d-bd14-5ea0-a642-453e7fc8146f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pi +3\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251725} {:answer/id #uuid "7abc315a-df85-5808-a5e3-431f90ce1561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+3\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf92c4b0-60ec-57c4-a752-cea1cf7e0fd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{15\\pi }{4}+6\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251724} :answer-field/id #uuid "127d56b5-3f60-57ec-9a4d-a8eb7e8d5cd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251721}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "82548378-f478-5059-a129-9b54510feafc" :question/math-academy-id "q-81536" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its minimum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the minima of\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe $X$-values at the minima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=(2n+1)\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the minima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =(2n+1)\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\pi +2\\pi n \\\\ \\frac{x}{3} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n \\\\ x & =\\frac{15\\pi }{4}+6\\pi n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that minimize $f(x)$ are given by $x=\\frac{15\\pi }{4}+6\\pi n,$ where $n$ is an integer."}]}
